---
import DocsLayout from '../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro';
import Aside from '../../../components/Aside.astro';
import { streamsSidebar } from '../../../data/streams-sidebar';
import { Code } from 'astro:components';
---

<DocsLayout title="Control & Timing" sidebar={streamsSidebar} description="Debounce, cutoff, ticker, and async mapping in Streams">

<h1>Control &amp; Timing</h1>

<p class="text-xl text-gray-400 !mt-2 !mb-8">
  Rate-limit, pause, and schedule stream emissions.
</p>

<h2>debounce</h2>

<p>Only publish after a quiet period. When values arrive rapidly, only the last one is emitted after the delay:</p>

<Code code={`val search = StreamSource("")
val debounced = search.debounce(300)  // 300ms delay

// Typing rapidly: "h", "he", "hel", "hell", "hello"
// Only "hello" is published — 300ms after the last keystroke`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<h2>cutoffWhen / cutoffWhenNot</h2>

<p>Pause a stream based on a boolean predicate stream. While paused, the source is fully unsubscribed. When resumed, the current value is published immediately:</p>

<Code code={`val source = StreamSource(1)
val paused = StreamSource(false)

val stream = source.cutoffWhen(paused)

// stream publishes values while paused() is false
source(2)  // published

paused(true)
source(3)  // NOT published — stream is cut off

paused(false)
// immediately publishes source's current value (3)`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<h2>ticker</h2>

<p>Emits a <code>TickerFrame</code> at regular intervals. Only runs while subscribed:</p>

    <Code code={`import io.peekandpoke.ultra.streams.ops.ticker

val tick = ticker(1000)  // every 1000ms

val unsub = tick.subscribeToStream { frame ->
    println("Tick #${'$'}{frame.count}, delta: ${'$'}{frame.deltaTime}ms")
}

// ... later
unsub()  // stops the ticker`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<p>Also accepts a <code>Duration</code>: <code>ticker(1.seconds)</code>, or any <code>FrameClock</code>:
    <code>ticker(clock)</code>, whose initial frame carries the time 0.0 and the next ones the clock's times. Times are
    in milliseconds, with fractions.</p>

<h2>FrameClock</h2>

<p>The clock a motion runs on: <code>next(onFrame)</code> calls back once, on the next frame, with the frame's time
    in milliseconds, and returns the way to cancel. <code>FrameClock.default</code> is the browser's animation frames
    on JS (<code>FrameClock.animationFrames</code>) and a 60 Hz <code>FrameClock.ticker()</code> elsewhere. A test
    passes its own clock and drives it frame by frame:</p>

    <Code code={`import io.peekandpoke.ultra.streams.ops.FrameClock

val handClock = FrameClock { onFrame ->
    pending = onFrame        // run it when the test says so
    ({ pending = null })     // cancel
}`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<p>On the JVM and native, <code>FrameClock.ticker(context = ...)</code> calls back on that context's threads: pick the one the
    source emits on, since streams are not thread-safe.</p>

<h2>smooth</h2>

<p>Smooths a <code>Stream&lt;Double?&gt;</code>: each new value is approached a step per frame, also while the source
    holds still, by one of the <a href="/ultra/maths/smoothing">Smoothing laws</a>. The default is exponential:</p>

    <Code code={`import io.peekandpoke.ultra.maths.Smoothing
import io.peekandpoke.ultra.streams.ops.smooth

// Keeps 90% of the way left per 10 ms
val needle = level.smooth(sharePer10Ms = 0.9)

// Any law: an analog needle that overshoots and settles
val analog = level.smooth(Smoothing.Spring(stiffness = 200.0, damping = 10.0), rest = 0.0, near = 0.001)`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<ul>
    <li>The first value after someone starts listening is taken as it is: a needle shown anew starts at its
        reading.</li>
    <li><code>null</code>, NaN and infinity pass at once as <code>null</code>; after a <code>null</code> the motion
        starts from <code>rest</code>.</li>
    <li>A value on the way becomes the new goal; the motion keeps its speed.</li>
    <li>Within <code>near</code> (in the value's units) it lands exactly on the value and stops asking for frames: no
        endless trickle of values.</li>
    <li>The source is listened to only while someone listens; no frame is asked for while nothing moves.</li>
</ul>

<p>The law can be a stream too, and change while the stream moves: the next step goes from the current position and
    speed by the new law, so a spring taking over swings on. A law of <code>null</code> passes values at once.</p>

    <Code code={`val law = StreamSource<Smoothing?>(Smoothing.Exponential(keepPer10Ms = 0.5))
val bulb = brightness.smooth(smoothing = law)

// A stutter: a new law, no restart of the motion
law(Smoothing.Spring(stiffness = 300.0, damping = 4.0))`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<h2>quantize</h2>

<p>Snaps every value to the nearest <code>offset + k * size</code>; the offset shifts the grid, not the value.
    <code>null</code> passes:</p>

    <Code code={`import io.peekandpoke.ultra.streams.ops.quantize

val stepped = level.smooth(sharePer10Ms = 0.9).quantize(size = 0.1)              // 0.0, 0.1, 0.2, ...
val shifted = level.quantize(size = 0.1, offset = 0.05)                          // 0.05, 0.15, ...`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<p>While a smoothed motion runs, <code>quantize</code> passes the same grid value on every frame; add
    <code>.distinct()</code> to publish only the changes.</p>

<h2>mapAsync</h2>

<p>Maps values asynchronously. The result stream is nullable — <code>null</code> until the first async operation completes:</p>

    <Aside>And there it is — the one place where "always has a value" meets async reality. The value is null until the
        coroutine finishes. We're not going to pretend otherwise.
    </Aside>

    <Code code={`val userId = StreamSource("user-1")

val user = userId.mapAsync { id ->
    api.fetchUser(id)  // suspend function
}

// user() is null initially, then the fetched User object`} lang="kotlin" theme="vitesse-dark" />

<div class="not-prose mt-8 flex gap-4">
  <a href="/ultra/streams/subscriptions" class="inline-flex items-center px-6 py-3 bg-brand-600 text-white font-semibold rounded-lg hover:bg-brand-500 transition-colors">
    Subscriptions &amp; resilience &rarr;
  </a>
</div>

</DocsLayout>
